    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`mock_openai()` in `tests/fakes.py` works again, and `pytest -q` passes (2 passed). Only `tests/fakes.py` changed; app/, the pytest configuration and the pinned packages are untouched.\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer uses `httpx`. It sends requests through a separate package, `httpx2`. The pinned respx (0.23.1) only knows how to intercept `httpx` and `httpcore`. So its mocks were active, but the OpenAI client's requests never passed through them, and nothing failed until the network call did.\n\n**Fix:** I added a small adapter class, `HTTPX2TransportMocker`, that plugs into respx's own extension point. `mock_openai()` now uses it via `respx.mock(..., using=HTTPX2_MOCKER)`.\n- It replaces the request-sending method on httpx2's `HTTPTransport` class itself, not on any one client. That's why clients created before the block, and `app.digest`'s cached client, are covered too.\n- It converts each request to `httpx` form, so the existing route and `answer` callback are unchanged, then converts the reply back.\n- respx puts the original method back when the `with` block exits.\n\nThe helper's interface and docstring are the same as before.\n\nI didn't use httpx2's built-in `alias_httpx()`: it affects the whole process and has to run before anything imports `httpx`, which a test helper can't guarantee.\n\n**Checks beyond the two tests:** I ran a throwaway script that confirmed each point in the docstring: the client made before the block, a client made inside it and the cached `digest` client all got the canned replies, a request past the last reply got the 400, and requests after the block are no longer intercepted. That last check wasn't offline, though. This shell had network access, so one request with a dummy key (`k`) reached the real api.openai.com and got back a 401 authentication error.\n\nOne cleanup item: I wasn't allowed to delete that script, so please remove `_check_fakes.py` from the project root yourself."
  },
